    {
      "id": "Ugz3dxudxjY_aKJnqox4AaABAg",
      "author": "@krisangel2100",
      "authorImage": "https://yt3.ggpht.com/ytc/AIdro_mlR9Va3Tb1Sot-8FQoiYFVPTyv6dRl_ReoSOroSqexKQ4=s48-c-k-c0x00ffffff-no-rj",
      "text": "In the highlands of Ratanakiri among the Krung, (3:17 mins of your video) this is a very small native tribe; probably a village of 30 families or less. Their village used to be far in the jungle isolated tribe, no Khmer/Cambodian could reach. Since the modern world cutting down the jungle become fields, that why we could visit them and learn more about their cultures. There are many minority tribes living differently; but most of them are adopted to Khmer and give up their tradition, languages... They used to be naked and refused to wear clothes with no money at all; only hunting, fishing and small farming to enjoy life. Young generations, don't even speak their own languages, some parents could; their grandparents could not even speak Khmer. \nAt 17:12 minutes, it is not in Cambodia at all. They don't live among any tombs; these are Christians and Catholics who built the cross for their deaths. I watched your video about the Philippines long time before about this the same one. Khmer hold Buddhism and Hinduism combination before the Angkor period thousand years ago, mostly cremation. So they build stupas in a pagoda to keep earns for memories of their ancestors. \nThank you for your kindly sharing knowlagues!",
      "likeCount": 0,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T02:39:39Z",
      "replyCount": 0,
      "replies": []
    },
    {
      "id": "UgwROKWdPart14Hm1nV4AaABAg",
